  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 352 PM EST THU NOV 12 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, 500 mb troughing will be \nlocated over the eastern CONUS, with the trough axis now east of \nthe forecast area. Farther west, a highly amplified ridge will be \ncentered over the Great Plains. At the surface, high pressure will\nbuild over the Midwest, with the original surface low now well \ninto the Atlantic. This high pressure will be the dominant player \nin eastern Kentucky's weather for the duration of the period, with\nlargely tranquil conditions expected.\n\nAlthough ridging will dominate the majority of the forecast \nperiod, some residual precipitation may linger on Saturday \nmorning. There is still some uncertainty regarding the quality of \nlingering moisture and cloud top temperatures, but with \nnorthwesterly flow persisting around the surface high, upslope \nwinds would still be favored. Thus, should ingredients align, \nadditional precipitation would be possible in southeastern \nKentucky, and pattern recognition suggests NBM PoPs may be \nslightly underdone, although precipitation chances remain very \nmarginal and limited. With a cold air mass still in place on \nSaturday morning, any precipitation would likely fall as snow in \nthe higher elevations, although if clouds are shallower with \nwarmer cloud tops, freezing drizzle could not be ruled out. \nRegardless, precipitation should cease as moisture profiles become\nless favorable and ridging continues to build east. Lingering \ncloud cover will keep highs below average, in the mid 40s for most\nlocations and colder for the highest terrain of southeastern \nKentucky. Should cloud cover linger into the night, lows could \nremain warmer than forecast for some locations; otherwise, \ntemperatures should range from the upper 20s to low 30s.\n\nFrom Sunday onward, weather will be tranquil and uneventful. \nAloft, the amplified 500 mb ridge will continue its eastward \nprogression, pushed toward the forecast area by a series of \nPacific disturbances. Around Monday, an upper level low will \ntraverse the Canadian Prairie provinces, which will flatten the \nridge somewhat, while in the Southern Plains, an additional low \nwill drift south into the Gulf; however, neither of these features\nwill have much impact on the pattern locally, with ridging \npersisting. 500 mb heights will continue to rise as the ridge axis\neventually makes its way overhead by midweek. This ridging will \nbe anomalously strong by the end of the period, with LREF mean 500\nmb heights rising to 581 dam, roughly the 90th percentile \ncompared to climatology. Meanwhile, at the surface, high pressure \nwill continue to shift east, with winds becoming increasingly \nsoutherly with time. This pattern will keep weather sunny and dry \nacross eastern Kentucky, with a general warming trend expected. \nAlthough highs will be below average (in the upper 40s and low \n50s) for the majority of the area on Sunday, maxTs look to rebound\ninto the 60s by the end of the period. With a dry air mass in \nplace, expect temperatures to cool off significantly overnight, \nwith lows generally ranging from the mid 20s to low 30s in \nvalleys, although overnight temperatures will remain warmer on \nridges.",
